#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
fruit_detector - finds ripe fruit in the survey camera and says where it is.

Two jobs, and the second is the one that is easy to get subtly wrong.

1. Decide which pixels are ripe fruit.
2. Turn those pixels into a position on the farm.

What "ripe" means to this node
------------------------------
Red, in HSV, and not much else. Hue near 0 (which wraps, so the test is two
bands), high saturation, decent value. The farm is built so that this is a real
discrimination rather than a trick: the unripe fruit is green and so is the
foliage, so a detector that merely found "objects" would report three times too
many. Hue separates them cleanly, which is exactly why colour spaces that put
hue on its own axis exist.

Saturation is the guard that matters most. Shadowed foliage and the grey ground
both drift towards low-saturation reds under some lighting, and a hue-only test
picks them up as a scatter of small blobs across the whole image. ~sat_min
throws those out; the fruit is a strongly saturated red and survives it.

Blobs, not pixels
-----------------
Contours, filtered by area. ~min_area_px rejects speckle - single red pixels
from compression or a lit edge - and ~max_area_px rejects anything too big to
be a berry, which in practice means two fruit touching in the image, or a
reflection. Both bounds are in pixels rather than metres because the thing
being filtered is an image artefact; the conversion to metres happens after.

From pixel to farm
------------------
This is the part with the sign errors in it, so the derivation is written out.

The camera looks straight down, mounted with rpy (0, pi/2, 0) - see
urdf/quadrotor_survey_cam.gazebo.xacro, which derives the same chain. In the
optical frame that mounting gives:

    image right (+u)  ->  body -y
    image down  (+v)  ->  body -x

A blob at pixel (u, v), with the camera's principal point (cx, cy) and focal
lengths (fx, fy) from camera_info, sits at

    dx_img = (u - cx) / fx        # tangent of the angle off-axis, image right
    dy_img = (v - cy) / fy        #                                image down

and at a range d along the optical axis those tangents become metres:

    body_x = -dy_img * d
    body_y = -dx_img * d

then rotate by the aircraft's yaw into the world and add its position.

Getting the range right
-----------------------
d is the camera's height above the apple, and there are two ways to get it.

The cheap one is a flat-plane assumption: the drone's altitude, less the
camera's mount offset, less ~fruit_plane_z. That is what this node used to do,
and on a field of uniform bushes it was fine. On apple trees it is not: fruit
hangs anywhere from 1.45 m to 2.46 m, so the assumed height is wrong by up to
half a metre. An error of e metres scales the offset from image centre by e/d,
so an apple 2 m off-axis at d = 3.4 m moves 0.29 m - which is more than the
gap between two apples on a tree, and enough to make a map worse than useless.

The better one is to measure it, and a monocular camera can, because the size
of the thing being looked at is known. An apple is ~0.11 m across, so

    d = fx * apple_diameter / apparent_diameter_px

with the apparent diameter taken from the blob's area as 2*sqrt(A/pi). This is
ordinary size-based ranging and it removes the flat-plane error entirely for
any apple whose blob is clean.

Which is the catch, so the two are used together. A partly occluded apple, or
two overlapping in the image, gives an area that is not the area of one whole
apple, and the range comes out wrong in a way nothing downstream could detect.
So the size estimate is used only when the blob is round enough to be one whole
apple (~circularity), and is clamped to a sensible band around the flat-plane
figure. Anything failing those falls back to the plane.

That is also still why ~leg_spacing defaults to the row spacing rather than the
wider coverage-optimal figure: whatever the range estimate, an apple near the
middle of the frame is less sensitive to every error in this chain.

Nothing here reads the ground truth. The detector gets an image, a camera
model, and the aircraft's pose.

Subscriptions
-------------
  image_raw             sensor_msgs/Image
  camera_info           sensor_msgs/CameraInfo
  ground_truth/state    nav_msgs/Odometry

Publications
------------
  /farm/sightings       uav_farm_survey/FruitSighting   one per blob per frame
  detections/image      sensor_msgs/Image               the annotated view

ROS Parameters (private, ~)
---------------------------
  ~hue_lo_max      int    Red wraps, so hue passes below this  (default 10)
  ~hue_hi_min      int    ...or above this                     (default 170)
  ~sat_min         int    Saturation floor; the main guard     (default 120)
  ~val_min         int    Value floor, against dark red shadow (default 60)
  ~min_area_px     float  Smallest blob worth believing        (default 30)
  ~max_area_px     float  Largest; above this it is two fruit  (default 4000)
  ~fruit_plane_z   float  Mean height fruit sits at, m; the
                          fallback when size ranging is not
                          usable                               (default 1.99)
  ~range_from_size bool   Estimate range from apparent apple
                          size rather than assuming the plane.
                          Measured worse on this orchard: an
                          apple is ~12 px across, so blob area
                          is noisy and every sighting lands in
                          a different place, splitting clusters
                          (precision 62.8% on, 92.7% off)      (default false)
  ~fruit_diameter  float  How big an apple is, m               (default 0.11)
  ~min_circularity float  Blobs rounder than this are treated
                          as one whole apple and get size
                          ranging; others fall back            (default 0.72)
  ~range_tol       float  Size range must stay within this
                          fraction of the plane estimate       (default 0.45)
  ~camera_offset_z float  Camera below base_link, m            (default 0.06)
  ~min_range       float  Ignore frames closer than this, m    (default 0.8)
  ~edge_margin_px  int    Ignore blobs this close to the frame
                          edge - a fruit half out of shot has
                          a centroid that is not its centre    (default 8)
  ~publish_debug   bool   Publish the annotated image          (default true)
"""

import math

import cv2
import numpy as np
import rospy
from cv_bridge import CvBridge
from nav_msgs.msg import Odometry
from sensor_msgs.msg import Image, CameraInfo
from tf.transformations import euler_from_quaternion

from uav_farm_survey.msg import FruitSighting


class FruitDetector(object):

    def __init__(self):
        rospy.init_node('fruit_detector', anonymous=False)
        self._ns = rospy.get_namespace().strip('/') or 'uav'

        self._hue_lo = int(rospy.get_param('~hue_lo_max', 10))
        self._hue_hi = int(rospy.get_param('~hue_hi_min', 170))
        self._sat_min = int(rospy.get_param('~sat_min', 120))
        self._val_min = int(rospy.get_param('~val_min', 60))
        self._min_area = rospy.get_param('~min_area_px', 30.0)
        self._max_area = rospy.get_param('~max_area_px', 4000.0)
        self._plane_z = rospy.get_param('~fruit_plane_z', 1.99)
        self._size_range = rospy.get_param('~range_from_size', False)
        self._fruit_d = rospy.get_param('~fruit_diameter', 0.11)
        self._min_circ = rospy.get_param('~min_circularity', 0.72)
        self._range_tol = rospy.get_param('~range_tol', 0.45)
        self._cam_dz = rospy.get_param('~camera_offset_z', 0.06)
        self._min_range = rospy.get_param('~min_range', 0.8)
        self._edge = int(rospy.get_param('~edge_margin_px', 8))
        self._debug = rospy.get_param('~publish_debug', True)

        self._bridge = CvBridge()
        self._K = None
        self._pose = None            # (x, y, z, yaw)
        self._frames = 0
        self._blobs = 0

        rospy.Subscriber('camera_info', CameraInfo, self._info_cb)
        rospy.Subscriber('ground_truth/state', Odometry, self._odom_cb)
        rospy.Subscriber('image_raw', Image, self._image_cb, queue_size=1,
                         buff_size=2 ** 24)

        self._pub = rospy.Publisher('/farm/sightings', FruitSighting,
                                    queue_size=50)
        self._dbg = (rospy.Publisher('detections/image', Image, queue_size=1)
                     if self._debug else None)

        rospy.Timer(rospy.Duration(5.0), self._report)
        rospy.loginfo('[%s] fruit detector up: ripe = hue<%d or >%d, sat>%d, '
                      'area %d-%d px', self._ns, self._hue_lo, self._hue_hi,
                      self._sat_min, int(self._min_area), int(self._max_area))

    # ----------------------------------------------------------- callbacks --
    def _info_cb(self, msg):
        # fx, fy, cx, cy. Taken from camera_info rather than recomputed from
        # the FOV and resolution, so that changing the lens in the xacro does
        # not silently leave this node projecting with the old numbers.
        self._K = (msg.K[0], msg.K[4], msg.K[2], msg.K[5])

    def _odom_cb(self, msg):
        p = msg.pose.pose.position
        q = msg.pose.pose.orientation
        _, _, yaw = euler_from_quaternion([q.x, q.y, q.z, q.w])
        self._pose = (p.x, p.y, p.z, yaw)

    def _report(self, _e):
        if self._frames:
            rospy.loginfo('[%s] %d frames, %d sightings (%.1f per frame)',
                          self._ns, self._frames, self._blobs,
                          float(self._blobs) / self._frames)

    # ------------------------------------------------------------ geometry --
    def _project(self, u, v, d):
        """Pixel -> world, on the assumed fruit plane. See the header.

        Returns None if the aircraft's pose or the camera model is not known
        yet, which happens for the first frame or two and is not worth a
        warning.
        """
        if self._K is None or self._pose is None:
            return None
        fx, fy, cx, cy = self._K
        px, py, _, yaw = self._pose

        dx_img = (u - cx) / fx          # image right
        dy_img = (v - cy) / fy          # image down

        body_x = -dy_img * d            # image down  -> body -x
        body_y = -dx_img * d            # image right -> body -y

        c, s = math.cos(yaw), math.sin(yaw)
        return (px + body_x * c - body_y * s,
                py + body_x * s + body_y * c)

    # --------------------------------------------------------- the detector --
    def _image_cb(self, msg):
        if self._pose is None or self._K is None:
            return
        d = self._pose[2] - self._cam_dz - self._plane_z
        if d < self._min_range:
            # Too low for the flat-plane projection to mean anything, and on
            # the ground it would report the landing gear.
            return

        try:
            bgr = self._bridge.imgmsg_to_cv2(msg, 'bgr8')
        except Exception as exc:
            rospy.logwarn_throttle(5.0, '[%s] cv_bridge: %s', self._ns, exc)
            return

        hsv = cv2.cvtColor(bgr, cv2.COLOR_BGR2HSV)
        # Red straddles the hue wrap-around, so it takes two bands. Everything
        # else in this farm - foliage, unripe fruit - is green, which is nowhere
        # near either of them.
        lo = cv2.inRange(hsv, (0, self._sat_min, self._val_min),
                              (self._hue_lo, 255, 255))
        hi = cv2.inRange(hsv, (self._hue_hi, self._sat_min, self._val_min),
                              (179, 255, 255))
        mask = cv2.bitwise_or(lo, hi)
        # Close small holes then drop speckle. Opening after closing, because
        # a berry with a specular highlight through the middle is one fruit,
        # not two, and joining it back up before measuring area is what keeps
        # it that way.
        k = np.ones((3, 3), np.uint8)
        mask = cv2.morphologyEx(mask, cv2.MORPH_CLOSE, k, iterations=2)
        mask = cv2.morphologyEx(mask, cv2.MORPH_OPEN, k, iterations=1)

        contours, _ = cv2.findContours(mask, cv2.RETR_EXTERNAL,
                                       cv2.CHAIN_APPROX_SIMPLE)
        h, w = mask.shape[:2]
        found = 0
        vis = None
        if self._dbg:
            # Tint everything the colour test accepted, so the debug view shows
            # what the detector is *thinking* and not only what it concluded.
            # A blob that is rejected on area or roundness still glows here,
            # which is the difference between "the threshold is wrong" and
            # "the filters are too tight" when a run goes badly.
            vis = bgr.copy()
            tint = np.zeros_like(vis)
            tint[:, :, 1] = mask
            tint[:, :, 2] = mask
            vis = cv2.addWeighted(vis, 1.0, tint, 0.35, 0)

        for c in contours:
            area = cv2.contourArea(c)
            if area < self._min_area or area > self._max_area:
                continue
            m = cv2.moments(c)
            if m['m00'] <= 0.0:
                continue
            u = m['m10'] / m['m00']
            v = m['m01'] / m['m00']
            if (u < self._edge or v < self._edge or
                    u > w - self._edge or v > h - self._edge):
                # Half out of frame: the centroid of what is visible is not the
                # centroid of the fruit, so the position would be biased inward
                # by an amount nothing downstream could detect. It will be seen
                # properly in the next frame or the next leg.
                continue

            # Range: measured from the blob if it looks like one whole apple,
            # assumed from the plane otherwise. See the header.
            d_use = d
            if self._size_range:
                per = cv2.arcLength(c, True)
                circ = (4.0 * math.pi * area / (per * per)) if per > 0 else 0.0
                if circ >= self._min_circ:
                    dia_px = 2.0 * math.sqrt(area / math.pi)
                    if dia_px > 1.0:
                        d_size = self._K[0] * self._fruit_d / dia_px
                        lo = d * (1.0 - self._range_tol)
                        hi = d * (1.0 + self._range_tol)
                        if lo <= d_size <= hi:
                            d_use = d_size

            world = self._project(u, v, d_use)
            if world is None:
                continue

            s = FruitSighting()
            s.header.stamp = msg.header.stamp
            s.header.frame_id = 'world'
            s.x, s.y = world[0], world[1]
            # The height this sighting implies, which is the aircraft's
            # altitude less the range actually used - so a size-ranged apple
            # reports where it really hangs rather than where the plane says.
            s.z = self._pose[2] - self._cam_dz - d_use
            s.u, s.v = int(u), int(v)
            s.area_px = float(area)
            s.range = d_use
            self._pub.publish(s)
            found += 1

            if vis is not None:
                # Ring the accepted apple, and label it with where the survey
                # thinks it is and how far away it decided it was. Green ring
                # when the range was measured from the blob, amber when it fell
                # back to the assumed plane - so a frame where the size ranging
                # is failing is obvious at a glance.
                col = (0, 255, 80) if d_use != d else (0, 190, 255)
                cv2.circle(vis, (int(u), int(v)), 13, col, 2)
                cv2.putText(vis, '%.1f,%.1f' % world, (int(u) + 15, int(v) - 2),
                            cv2.FONT_HERSHEY_SIMPLEX, 0.38, col, 1)
                cv2.putText(vis, '%.2fm' % d_use, (int(u) + 15, int(v) + 12),
                            cv2.FONT_HERSHEY_SIMPLEX, 0.34, col, 1)

        self._frames += 1
        self._blobs += found

        if vis is not None:
            cv2.rectangle(vis, (0, 0), (w, 46), (0, 0, 0), -1)
            cv2.putText(vis, 'RIPE IN FRAME: %d' % found, (8, 20),
                        cv2.FONT_HERSHEY_SIMPLEX, 0.62, (0, 255, 80), 2)
            cv2.putText(vis, 'alt %.2f m over plane | total %d in %d frames'
                        % (d, self._blobs + found, self._frames + 1),
                        (8, 39), cv2.FONT_HERSHEY_SIMPLEX, 0.44, (200, 200, 200), 1)
            # Crosshair: the one pixel where the projection has no lever arm at
            # all, so it is worth being able to see what is under it.
            cv2.drawMarker(vis, (w // 2, h // 2), (255, 255, 255),
                           cv2.MARKER_CROSS, 18, 1)
            try:
                # Built by hand rather than with cv_bridge.cv2_to_imgmsg,
                # which raises KeyError: 16 on this install - a cv_bridge /
                # OpenCV version mismatch in its cvtype_to_name table that
                # hits every bgr8 image, not just some. imgmsg_to_cv2 on the
                # way in is unaffected, so only the outbound leg needs this.
                # A bgr8 Image is a header, a shape and the bytes; there is
                # nothing cv_bridge does here that is worth the dependency.
                out = Image()
                out.header = msg.header
                out.height, out.width = vis.shape[:2]
                out.encoding = 'bgr8'
                out.is_bigendian = 0
                out.step = out.width * 3
                out.data = vis.tobytes()
                self._dbg.publish(out)
            except Exception as exc:
                # Never swallow this silently. A debug view that fails quietly
                # looks exactly like a debug view showing nothing interesting,
                # and the difference costs a whole run to notice.
                rospy.logwarn_throttle(5.0, '[%s] debug view: %s',
                                       self._ns, exc)


def main():
    FruitDetector()
    rospy.spin()


if __name__ == '__main__':
    main()
